1 DUMP
    The DUMP command shows a file on a mounted volume in hexadecimal and as
    text, as VMS's DUMP does: a virtual block at a time (the default, up
    to the file's end-of-file block), or with /RECORD a record at a time,
    each with its record's file address (RFA) and without a VFC record's
    fixed control.  Each line shows 32 bytes: eight longwords in
    hexadecimal, the highest first, so that each reads as a number; then
    the bytes as text, lowest first, "." for one that isn't printable; then
    the offset of the line's first byte.

    Syntax:

        DUMP [/BLOCKS[=range] | /RECORD[=range]] file-spec

    Parameter:

        file-spec       One file on a mounted volume (no wildcards); the
                        newest version unless a version is given.

    Qualifiers:

        /BLOCKS[=(START:n, END:n, COUNT:n)]
                        Show the file's blocks, from START (1) to END or
                        for COUNT blocks.

        /RECORD[=(START=n, END=n, COUNT=n)]
                        Show the file's records, numbered from 1.

    Examples:

        DUMP/BLOCKS=COUNT:1 LOGIN.COM
        DUMP/RECORD=(START=10,COUNT=5) DATA.DAT
